US President Donald Trump has apparently concluded that a trip to Moscow is the universal key to resolving the largest armed conflict in Europe since 1945. According to Bloomberg, during a meeting on the sidelines of the UN General Assembly, the US leader again urged Volodymyr Zelenskyy to travel to the Russian capital for direct negotiations with Vladimir Putin.

The Ukrainian president categorically rejected the proposal. According to the agency, Trump’s mere revival of the idea to hold talks on enemy territory provoked visible irritation from Zelenskyy.

Despite the overt friction, European diplomats characterized the New York meeting as “generally positive.” In the behind-the-scenes lexicon of high-stakes diplomacy, this likely indicates only that the two leaders shook hands and neither abruptly walked out before the scheduled conclusion. The White House, adhering to custom, declined to comment.

